Exactly. And Tourette’s gives you a particularly interesting nearby mechanism—but with an important distinction: a tic is not simply a compulsion. Many people with Tourette syndrome experience a premonitory urge before a tic, and the tic can alter or relieve that sensation. CBIT exploits that sequence by training awareness of the warning signal and inserting a competing response. OCD-type compulsions can have superficially similar tension/action/relief loops, but their phenomenology and underlying disorder are not identical. 

What you’re describing beyond that is almost a Sensorium Ablation / Latent Reconstruction method:

\text{hidden world state } Z
\rightarrow
\{V,A,T,\ldots\}
\rightarrow
\text{observer's reconstruction }\hat Z

Normally you receive vision + sound + language + context simultaneously and your brain silently fuses them into “what happened.” Instead, deliberately delete one channel:

\hat Z_V,\quad \hat Z_A,\quad \hat Z_{V+A},\quad \hat Z_{\text{full}}

Then compare them.

The fascinating quantity isn’t merely whether you guessed correctly. It’s:

\Delta_i=\hat Z_i-\hat Z_{\text{full}}

because that tells you what each sensory channel caused you to invent, miss, overweight, or correctly recover.

That gives you several useful combinations:

1. Silent-film inference. Watch an interaction muted. Predict relationships, turn-taking, dominance, disagreement, affection, surprise, who knows what, and what happens next. Then restore sound. Your errors reveal your visual social priors.
2. Audio-only reconstruction. Hide the picture. Infer spacing, movement, number of people, object interactions, emotional dynamics, room geometry, and forthcoming actions. Restore video afterward. You’re effectively learning an acoustic grammar of events.
3. Semantic deprivation. Keep speech prosody but remove intelligible language—foreign-language footage works well if you genuinely don’t understand it. Now rhythm, timing, interruption, pitch, laughter, gesture, gaze and synchronization become much louder informational channels.
4. Cross-modal translation. Ask, “What is the auditory equivalent of this visual pattern?” Movement accelerating might correspond to rising tempo; spatial distance to loudness/reverberation; conversational interruption to visual occlusion. This becomes a library of functional equivalents rather than literal equivalents. Research on sensory loss shows that the brain can substantially reorganize how remaining sensory information is processed, although the consequences are not simply “one sense disappears, every other sense gets better.” 
5. Urge topology. For something like a tic, habit, craving, or repetitive behavior, don’t begin with the visible action. Map the preceding sequence: sensation → location in body → intensity → context → prediction → action → immediate consequence → later consequence. In Tourette treatment, awareness of premonitory urges plus an incompatible response is already clinically useful; CBIT is an established behavioral treatment rather than mere forced suppression. 
6. Multimodal rehabilitation/assistive systems. This could become genuinely useful online: teach somebody a missing or unreliable channel through another channel. Instead of “fixing the person,” you’re giving them another interface to the same underlying information. Visual event → haptic pattern; speech rhythm → visualization; body-state warning → discreet vibration; uncertain social interpretation → alternative hypotheses rather than a confident guessed story.

And there is an important epistemic safeguard embedded in your idea: the story you reconstruct is not necessarily the underlying story.

Two people can see exactly the same shoulder movement:

O=\text{shoulder movement}

while the hidden causes could be:

Z \in \{\text{tic, pain, stretch, signal, habit, anxiety, joke, cold,\ldots}\}

So instead of the system saying “I know what that person is thinking,” the stronger system maintains a distribution:

P(Z\mid O)

and then actively looks for the observation that would most efficiently discriminate among those hypotheses.

That’s almost scientific method performed perceptually.

And your “set it down, get coffee, and watch” idea adds another trick: observer decoupling. You’re no longer participating in the scene and constantly steering it. You can watch trajectories, recurrence, synchronization, collision, avoidance, clustering, attention shifts, turn-taking, and cause/effect unfold over time.

Combine that with the ablation method and you get something stronger:

full reality → remove a channel → reconstruct → predict → restore the channel → measure delta → extract the useful perceptual operator → try it in another domain.

That’s a very clean addition to the representation-surfing machinery we’ve been building: instead of merely translating representations, you deliberately damage the representation in controlled ways to discover what was holding it together.